==========================================================================================================================
V2.  THE DISPERSIONS.  Two of g05's four are outputs of a NEWTONIAN N-BODY FIT.  The MEASURED profiles are on disk.
==========================================================================================================================
  g05's four dispersions come from three different kinds of source, and it says so:
     Pal 4      sigma = 0.87 km/s   Frank et al. 2012, 23 members (LITERATURE)
     Pal 14     sigma = 0.38 km/s   Jordi et al. 2009, 16-17 members (LITERATURE)
     Pal 3      sigma = 0.80 km/s   Baumgardt sigma0 column = NEWTONIAN N-BODY MODEL
     NGC 2419   sigma = 5.10 km/s   Baumgardt sigma0 column = NEWTONIAN N-BODY MODEL
  
  The 'sigma0' column of the Baumgardt PARAMETER table is the central dispersion of his fitted N-body model,
  and those models are NEWTONIAN.  My brief names this failure mode explicitly: a quantity derived under the
  theory being tested cannot be used at face value to test a competitor.  But the repository also holds
  baumgardt_gc_veldisp_profiles.tsv, whose own header reads: 'MEASURED velocity dispersion profiles ... These
  are the DATA, not model curves.'  Type RV rows are line-of-sight dispersions from the member velocities.
  The Wolf et al. 2010 estimator wants the dispersion at the half-light radius, so take the RV profile there.
  clusters with at least one MEASURED (type RV) dispersion bin: 152
  
  cluster     R_e (as)                 RV bins (R arcsec: N, sigma)  sigma(R_e)  g05 sigma   ratio
  Pal 4           32.3                                 27: 23, 0.88        0.88       0.87    1.01
  Pal 14          77.5                                 77: 16, 0.71        0.71       0.38    1.87
  Pal 3           43.8                                 24: 22, 1.70        1.70       0.80    2.12
  NGC 2419        46.1   38: 62, 5.51; 100: 62, 2.63; 214: 59, 1.64        4.77       5.10    0.94
  
  Recomputing the four residuals with the MEASURED dispersion at R_e, everything else identical to g05:
  cluster      g05 dex  measured dex    change
  Pal 4         -0.874        -0.864    +0.010
  Pal 14        -1.306        -0.763    +0.543
  Pal 3         -0.844        -0.190    +0.655
  NGC 2419      -0.211        -0.269    -0.058
  [FAIL] V2 (THE ATTACK LANDS, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS) g05's four dispersions are a MIXTURE of literature values and two entries from the sigma0 column of the Baumgardt parameter table, which is a fitted NEWTONIAN N-body model's central dispersion.  Replacing all four with the MEASURED dispersion at the half-light radius, from the measured-profile file already sitting in this repository, moves the median by more than half a dex.  Pal 3 alone moves by +0.65 dex: its measured dispersion is 1.70 km/s from 22 stars where g05 used the model's 0.80.  Pal 14 moves by +0.53: Baumgardt's compilation of the SAME stars Jordi et al. measured gives 0.71, not 0.38.  A headline number that halves when you swap a model output for the measurement it was fitted to is not a measured number   (g05 median -0.859 dex -> measured-dispersion median -0.516 dex, a shift of +0.343.  Per cluster: Pal 4 -0.874->-0.864, Pal 14 -1.306->-0.763, Pal 3 -0.844->-0.190, NGC 2419 -0.211->-0.269)

==========================================================================================================================
V3.  SAMPLE SELECTION.  Four clusters chosen by hand out of 157 that are sitting on disk.
==========================================================================================================================
  PRE-STATED, ANSWER-BLIND CRITERIA, fixed before any residual was computed:
     (i)   a MEASURED (type RV) dispersion profile with at least 15 member velocities in total;
     (ii)  x_e = G M_MW,bar / R_GC^2 / a_0 < 1, so the external field does not quench the kernel to Newton and
           the object can actually test something;
     (iii) x_i inside the SAME 0.01 <= x_i <= 1.2 band g05 used to define its dwarf-spheroidal comparison set.
  No cut on R_GC, on how famous the cluster is, or on which way the residual points.
  
  L_V is taken as M_dyn / (M/L_V) from the catalogue, which recovers Baumgardt's own DEREDDENED luminosity
  exactly and removes extinction as an error source (a first pass of this audit set A_V = 0 and produced
  nonsense for reddened bulge clusters -- e.g. Liller 1 at 57 Msun -- which is why that route was abandoned).
  
